{"reference":"Ephesians 3","book":"Ephesians","chapter":3,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"The disclosed mystery","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The “mystery” Paul describes is not hidden knowledge reserved for an elite group. He states it plainly: Gentiles are fellow-heirs, fellow-members of one body, and fellow-partakers of the promise in Christ through the gospel. The chapter’s central concern is this disclosed shared standing, alongside Paul’s presentation of himself as an apostle and prisoner; it does not narrate how the letter was composed.","verse_refs":["Ephesians 3:1","Ephesians 3:1-13"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_ephesians_003_e510223430ea4ca067c85a6e"]}]},{"kind":"cultural_context","title":"Reading the chapter carefully","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"Several phrases can be misunderstood if detached from their explanation in the chapter. “Mystery” names the revealed inclusion of Gentiles, not a basis for conspiracy theories, secret elites, or hostility to learning. Paul’s imprisonment should also not be romanticized as though suffering itself establishes authority. His prayer for inward strength belongs alongside, rather than in place of, medicine, mental-health care, safeguarding, and practical help.","verse_refs":["Ephesians 3:1","Ephesians 3:1-13","Ephesians 3:14-21"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_ephesians_003_5ffb061b4a1f867bd58b1ccf"]}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Questions the chapter leaves open","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"The addressees are treated as Gentiles, but the chapter does not disclose their city, assembly structure, or full demographics. Its first-person voice identifies Paul, yet direct Pauline authorship remains substantially debated, with Pauline, secretary-mediated, associate, and school proposals still proposed. Suggested dates, from the early 60s to roughly 70–90 CE, depend chiefly on those authorship models rather than on an explicit date in the text.","verse_refs":["Ephesians 3:1","Ephesians 3:1-13","Ephesians 3:10"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_ephesians_003_3397b8006eafb65a41418bff"]},{"id":"block_4","text":"Verse 10 says that the church makes God’s wisdom known to “principalities and powers in the heavenly places.” The referents and cosmology are debated. These terms may involve cosmic and political dimensions, but treating human opponents as demonic can foster scapegoating and violence.","verse_refs":["Ephesians 3:10"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_ephesians_003_5d50aa4f0e7398fec7a961ae"]}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"A related letter","blocks":[{"id":"block_5","text":"Ephesians and Colossians share extensive language and structure. How that relationship arose, and which writing may depend on the other, remains debated.","verse_refs":["Ephesians 1:1-23","Ephesians 2:1-22","Ephesians 4:1-32","Ephesians 5:1-33","Ephesians 6:1-24"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_ephesians_003_9f81abdef6580d861335e54e"]}]}],"generated_metadata":null}